## Dashboard Integration Examples

### React/TypeScript: Pending Returns Component

```typescript
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

interface Return {
  id: string;
  return_number: string;
  status: string;
  customer: {
    first_name: string;
    last_name: string;
    email: string;
  };
  order: {
    order_number: string;
  };
  total_refund_amount: number;
  currency: string;
  requested_at: string;
}

export function PendingReturns() {
  const [returns, setReturns] = useState<Return[]>([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

  useEffect(() => {
    fetchPendingReturns();
  }, []);

  const fetchPendingReturns = async () => {
    try {
      const response = await fetch('http://localhost:3100/server/return/query', {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
        body: JSON.stringify({
          status: 'REQUESTED',
          expand: 'customer,order',
          orderBy: 'requested_at',
          ascending: true,
          limit: 50
        })
      });

      const result = await response.json();
      if (result.success) {
        setReturns(result.data.returns);
      }
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Error fetching pending returns:', error);
    } finally {
      setLoading(false);
    }
  };

  if (loading) return <div>Loading...</div>;

  return (
    <div className="pending-returns">
      <h2>Pending Returns ({returns.length})</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Return #</th>
            <th>Customer</th>
            <th>Order #</th>
            <th>Refund Amount</th>
            <th>Requested</th>
            <th>Actions</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {returns.map(ret => (
            <tr key={ret.id}>
              <td>{ret.return_number}</td>
              <td>{ret.customer.first_name} {ret.customer.last_name}</td>
              <td>{ret.order.order_number}</td>
              <td>${(ret.total_refund_amount / 100).toFixed(2)}</td>
              <td>{new Date(ret.requested_at).toLocaleDateString()}</td>
              <td>
                <button onClick={() => handleApprove(ret.id)}>Approve</button>
                <button onClick={() => handleReject(ret.id)}>Reject</button>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### React/TypeScript: Returns Search Component

```typescript
import { useState } from 'react';

export function ReturnsSearch() {
  const [searchTerm, setSearchTerm] = useState('');
  const [results, setResults] = useState([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);

  const handleSearch = async () => {
    if (!searchTerm.trim()) return;

    setLoading(true);
    try {
      const response = await fetch('http://localhost:3100/server/return/query', {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
        body: JSON.stringify({
          search: searchTerm,
          expand: 'customer,order,items',
          limit: 50
        })
      });

      const result = await response.json();
      if (result.success) {
        setResults(result.data.returns);
      }
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Error searching returns:', error);
    } finally {
      setLoading(false);
    }
  };

  return (
    <div className="returns-search">
      <div className="search-bar">
        <input
          type="text"
          value={searchTerm}
          onChange={(e) => setSearchTerm(e.target.value)}
          onKeyPress={(e) => e.key === 'Enter' && handleSearch()}
          placeholder="Search by return number, order number, tracking..."
        />
        <button onClick={handleSearch} disabled={loading}>
          {loading ? 'Searching...' : 'Search'}
        </button>
      </div>

      {results.length > 0 && (
        <div className="search-results">
          <h3>Found {results.length} returns</h3>
          {/* Render results */}
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Performance Notes

### Indexing
The returns table should have indexes on:
- `return_number` (unique, frequently searched)
- `"order"` (foreign key, frequently filtered)
- `customer` (foreign key, frequently filtered)
- `status` (frequently filtered)
- `requested_at` (frequently sorted)
- `completed_at` (frequently filtered)

### Search Performance
- Client-side search is performed after database query
- For large result sets, consider limiting the initial query with filters
- Search is case-insensitive and matches partial strings
- Search covers: return_number, tracking_number, customer data, order data, notes

### Expansion Performance
- Use expansions judiciously - each expansion adds join overhead
- `items.variant` (nested expansion) is more expensive than `items`
- For large datasets, consider paginating with smaller limits

## Best Practices

### 1. Always Use Filters When Possible
Instead of querying all returns and filtering client-side, use database filters:

```bash
# Good: Filter at database level
curl -X POST http://localhost:3100/server/return/query \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"status": "REQUESTED", "customer": "customer-uuid"}'

# Avoid: Fetching all and filtering client-side
curl -X POST http://localhost:3100/server/return/query \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"limit": 1000}'
```

### 2. Use Date Ranges for Reports
Leverage date filters for efficient reporting:

```bash
# Get returns for current month
curl -X POST http://localhost:3100/server/return/query \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "requested_after": "2026-01-01",
    "requested_before": "2026-01-31",
    "expand": "customer,order"
  }'
```

### 3. Expand Only What You Need
Don't expand relations unless you need the data:

```bash
# If you only need return status, don't expand
curl -X POST http://localhost:3100/server/return/query \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"status": "REQUESTED"}'

# Expand only when displaying detailed information
curl -X POST http://localhost:3100/server/return/query \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "status": "REQUESTED",
    "expand": "customer,order,items.variant"
  }'
```

### 4. Use Pagination for Large Datasets
Always paginate when dealing with many returns:

```bash
curl -X POST http://localhost:3100/server/return/query \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "page": 1,
    "limit": 50,
    "orderBy": "requested_at",
    "ascending": false
  }'
```

### 5. Combine Filters for Specific Queries
Combine multiple filters for precise results:

```bash
# Find completed returns awaiting refund for specific customer
curl -X POST http://localhost:3100/server/return/query \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "customer": "customer-uuid",
    "status": "COMPLETED",
    "refund_processed": false,
    "expand": "order"
  }'
```

